   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to information on service\nlines to be reported by public water systems",
    "basePrintNo": "S8055",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8055-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 31,
       "fullName": "Robert Jackson",
       "imgName": "1050_Robert_Jackson.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1230,
       "sessionMemberId": 2017,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "JACKSON"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1114-b, Pub Health L (as proposed in S.5512 & A.6115)",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8055\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to information on\nservice lines to be reported by public water systems\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis is a chapter amendment that makes changes to provisions of L.2023,\nc.730, to enact the lead pipe right to know act requiring public water\nsystems to take service line inventories and make such information\navailable to the public.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis legislation amends the underlying chapter by tying state statutory\ndefinitions of \"parts\" used in service lines which are reported in\nservice line inventories and mapping to match federal regulations, in\ncase the federal EPA changes their regulations, to ensure continued\nstatutory alignment. It changes the scope of applicability of a \"covered\npublic water system\" to mean a public water system that services at\nleast 15 service connections. It pushes out the requirement to publish\nan interactive map and inventory summary form documents online from 90\ndays to 120 days. This is to allow for the DOH to seek federal funding\nand use it to hire a contractor to develop the map. It adds language\nrequiring the DOH to post a link to NYC's website in DOH's website. It\nalso adds language to exempt public water systems serving more than ten\nthousand people and fewer than 2 million people - since NYC already has\nits own program, which is already acknowledged in the bill. Lastly, it\nmoves the placement of the \"notwithstanding\" clause to clarify that\nwhile water utilities are required to include the data points in their\ninventories, they are also still required to comply with any regulations\nfrom the EPA on the topic.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation is a negotiated change to the underlying chapter.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter Amendment to Chapter 730 of the Laws of 2023. 5.5512 and A.6115\nboth passed the Senate and Assembly in April and June 2023, respective-\nly.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as a\nchapter of the laws of 2023 amending the public health law relating to\nenacting the lead pipe right to know act, as proposed in legislative\nbills numbers S. 5512 and A. 6115, takes effect.\n",
